1
00:00:00,270 --> 00:00:03,470
All right, final section of this chapter, wound healing.

2
00:00:04,210 --> 00:00:07,290
The idea here, and the reason why I sort of placed this

3
00:00:07,290 --> 00:00:08,670
at the very end of this chapter,

4
00:00:08,890 --> 00:00:11,310
is that we've been talking about inflammation,

5
00:00:11,690 --> 00:00:14,570
but we should remember that any time we have inflammation,

6
00:00:14,970 --> 00:00:16,850
as soon as inflammation begins,

7
00:00:17,330 --> 00:00:20,290
we also initiate a process called healing.

8
00:00:20,910 --> 00:00:24,170
And the idea here is that inflammation is a destructive process.

9
00:00:24,430 --> 00:00:25,370
It damages tissues.

10
00:00:25,810 --> 00:00:27,250
And any time we damage tissues,

11
00:00:27,250 --> 00:00:28,750
we're going to want to heal those tissues.

12
00:00:28,750 --> 00:00:33,510
And so healing is a consequence or a subsequent consequence of inflammation.

13
00:00:34,230 --> 00:00:37,190
Now, it's important to note that healing actually occurs via two processes.

14
00:00:37,410 --> 00:00:38,890
It's usually a combination of both.

15
00:00:39,650 --> 00:00:46,050
Regeneration, which refers to healing so that we actually replace the damaged tissue

16
00:00:46,050 --> 00:00:49,510
with the tissue itself by regenerating it, or repair.

17
00:00:49,970 --> 00:00:54,650
And the idea with repair is that instead of replacing the tissue with itself,

18
00:00:54,650 --> 00:01:00,390
we replace the tissue with a scar, all right, with collagen and fibrosis.

19
00:01:00,470 --> 00:01:02,670
And so usually when we have healing,

20
00:01:02,770 --> 00:01:05,730
it occurs by a combination of both regeneration and repair.

21
00:01:06,450 --> 00:01:08,570
Now, again, as I've stated, what is regeneration?

22
00:01:09,210 --> 00:01:12,270
It's replacement of the damaged tissue with the native tissue.

23
00:01:13,270 --> 00:01:15,230
And, I mean, that would be the ideal.

24
00:01:15,350 --> 00:01:20,070
Anytime you have damage, you would obviously prefer to replace it with native tissue.

25
00:01:20,070 --> 00:01:21,950
But we can't always do that.

26
00:01:22,470 --> 00:01:27,550
And regeneration, it's important to note that it's dependent on the regenerative capacity of the tissue.

27
00:01:28,050 --> 00:01:33,410
So this then leads to a classic examiner's type of topic that you should be familiar with,

28
00:01:33,690 --> 00:01:38,370
which is that tissues can be divided into three types based on their regenerative capacity.

29
00:01:38,990 --> 00:01:41,890
So the first type of tissue are called labile tissues.

30
00:01:42,830 --> 00:01:43,850
What are labile tissues?

31
00:01:44,170 --> 00:01:46,130
These are tissues that are constantly cycling.

32
00:01:46,130 --> 00:01:50,830
Basically, the tissue has stem cells, and the stem cells are constantly regenerating the tissue,

33
00:01:51,310 --> 00:01:53,290
just as part of our normal physiology.

34
00:01:53,710 --> 00:01:56,410
For example, our bowel is constantly regenerating.

35
00:01:56,930 --> 00:01:59,170
The skin is constantly regenerating.

36
00:01:59,510 --> 00:02:03,590
The red blood cells, the white blood cells, the platelets are constantly regenerating.

37
00:02:03,990 --> 00:02:05,790
So these are examples of labile tissues,

38
00:02:06,010 --> 00:02:09,630
and they have the ability to regenerate tissue when they're damaged.

39
00:02:10,070 --> 00:02:12,890
So, for example, let's just take the skin.

40
00:02:12,890 --> 00:02:18,370
If you were to get a paper cut and you were to look for the paper cut two years later,

41
00:02:18,450 --> 00:02:19,530
you wouldn't be able to find it.

42
00:02:19,570 --> 00:02:21,470
In fact, you wouldn't be able to find it a couple days later.

43
00:02:21,550 --> 00:02:23,810
You wouldn't even remember it a week later.

44
00:02:24,210 --> 00:02:29,790
And that's because you basically knock out the superficial layer of the epithelium,

45
00:02:30,250 --> 00:02:34,090
but you have stem cells, which are called basal cells,

46
00:02:34,350 --> 00:02:38,370
which have the ability to regenerate the tissue completely.

47
00:02:38,770 --> 00:02:40,910
And so you'll get regeneration of the tissue.

48
00:02:40,910 --> 00:02:42,650
Same thing with the small and large bowel.

49
00:02:42,710 --> 00:02:46,190
When a patient gets diarrhea, you don't need to, you know,

50
00:02:46,230 --> 00:02:48,550
gets an infection in the GI tract and gets diarrhea.

51
00:02:48,730 --> 00:02:51,290
You don't worry about transplanting the bowel or something like that

52
00:02:51,290 --> 00:02:54,870
because the bowel basically has the ability to regenerate itself.

53
00:02:55,450 --> 00:02:56,990
So that's the first type of tissue.

54
00:02:57,190 --> 00:03:03,370
And here we can get into a little bit of histology,

55
00:03:03,850 --> 00:03:05,030
and the examiners like to,

56
00:03:05,110 --> 00:03:07,630
by reminding ourselves where the stem cells are actually located.

57
00:03:07,630 --> 00:03:11,690
So, for example, if I asked you where are the stem cells in the small and large bowel,

58
00:03:12,050 --> 00:03:13,670
the answer would be in the crypts.

59
00:03:14,010 --> 00:03:14,910
You need to know that.

60
00:03:15,370 --> 00:03:17,370
If I asked you where are the stem cells in the skin,

61
00:03:17,450 --> 00:03:19,290
the answer would be in the basal layer,

62
00:03:19,430 --> 00:03:23,190
which is the very lowest layer, the most inner layer of the epidermis.

63
00:03:23,570 --> 00:03:24,470
You need to know that.

64
00:03:24,830 --> 00:03:26,550
What's the stem cell of the bone marrow?

65
00:03:26,870 --> 00:03:28,370
The hematopoietic stem cell.

66
00:03:28,770 --> 00:03:31,450
Now, we can't see the hematopoietic stem cell on histology,

67
00:03:31,450 --> 00:03:34,290
but do you know the marker of the hematopoietic stem cell,

68
00:03:34,470 --> 00:03:36,930
which is very high yield, shows up on exams all the time,

69
00:03:37,050 --> 00:03:38,890
used in clinics when you do transplants?

70
00:03:39,870 --> 00:03:40,290
Do you know it?

71
00:03:40,650 --> 00:03:43,030
Okay, CD34.

72
00:03:43,670 --> 00:03:44,550
You've got to know that.

73
00:03:45,230 --> 00:03:48,150
Bone marrow stem cells are CD34 positive.

74
00:03:48,330 --> 00:03:49,490
You will use this in clinics,

75
00:03:49,610 --> 00:03:52,410
especially when you do autologous stem cell transplanting,

76
00:03:52,770 --> 00:03:56,190
and you need to know this for examinations as well.

77
00:03:56,650 --> 00:03:58,710
Not listed here, but I could ask you another question.

78
00:03:58,710 --> 00:04:01,950
What's the stem cell of the lung, of the alveoli of the lung?

79
00:04:02,690 --> 00:04:03,090
Excellent.

80
00:04:03,290 --> 00:04:04,190
Type 2 pneumocytes.

81
00:04:04,310 --> 00:04:05,030
You need to know that.

82
00:04:05,110 --> 00:04:07,170
The type 2 pneumocyte is the stem cell of the lung.

83
00:04:07,390 --> 00:04:08,830
We'll talk about type 2 pneumocytes

84
00:04:08,830 --> 00:04:10,170
when we do the lung lecture, however.

85
00:04:10,750 --> 00:04:12,070
Anyway, that's labile tissues.

86
00:04:12,830 --> 00:04:14,850
I put this picture in here to remind you

87
00:04:14,850 --> 00:04:16,290
where the stem cells are in the bowel

88
00:04:16,290 --> 00:04:17,710
because they ask it on exams.

89
00:04:18,130 --> 00:04:19,990
And so these are the crypts down here,

90
00:04:20,090 --> 00:04:22,370
way at the bottom of these regions like this.

91
00:04:22,410 --> 00:04:23,010
This is the crypt.

92
00:04:23,270 --> 00:04:26,870
And the stem cells basically sit at the very base of the crypts.

93
00:04:26,870 --> 00:04:29,210
That's where the stem cells of the bowel are located.

94
00:04:29,670 --> 00:04:30,950
Here's a picture of the skin.

95
00:04:31,450 --> 00:04:33,090
This is the epidermis.

96
00:04:33,170 --> 00:04:34,290
This is the dermis.

97
00:04:34,670 --> 00:04:38,130
And you know that the epidermis consists of keratinocytes,

98
00:04:38,290 --> 00:04:40,110
and there's this dark layer here

99
00:04:40,110 --> 00:04:42,050
that you can kind of see running along the base,

100
00:04:42,690 --> 00:04:44,610
and that is the basal layer.

101
00:04:44,890 --> 00:04:46,730
I'm going to erase this so that you can now see

102
00:04:46,730 --> 00:04:48,370
that this little dark blue layer,

103
00:04:48,870 --> 00:04:50,390
this represents the basal layer.

104
00:04:50,730 --> 00:04:54,230
And the basal layer is the regenerative stem cell layer of the skin.

105
00:04:54,230 --> 00:04:56,830
All right, stable tissues.

106
00:04:56,930 --> 00:04:57,750
What are stable tissues?

107
00:04:57,910 --> 00:04:59,530
These are tissues that are quiescent.

108
00:04:59,710 --> 00:05:00,250
They're stable.

109
00:05:00,370 --> 00:05:02,250
They sort of sit outside of cell cycle.

110
00:05:02,450 --> 00:05:04,790
But they have the ability to reenter cell cycle.

111
00:05:05,090 --> 00:05:07,370
And this is the second type of tissue present in the body.

112
00:05:07,850 --> 00:05:10,210
The classic example of this would be the liver,

113
00:05:10,630 --> 00:05:13,950
so that if you actually partially resect the liver,

114
00:05:14,490 --> 00:05:16,570
you can get regeneration of the liver,

115
00:05:16,870 --> 00:05:19,210
and that occurs by compensatory hyperplasia.

116
00:05:19,550 --> 00:05:22,090
So what happens is when there's partial resection of the liver,

117
00:05:22,090 --> 00:05:25,810
each of the hepatocytes present within the liver

118
00:05:25,810 --> 00:05:29,590
has the ability to come out of quiescence

119
00:05:29,590 --> 00:05:31,910
and to reenter cell cycle.

120
00:05:32,370 --> 00:05:33,830
And when it reenters the cell cycle,

121
00:05:33,930 --> 00:05:37,130
it can divide and allow for regeneration of the liver.

122
00:05:37,650 --> 00:05:40,970
And so this is a very high-yield example of a stable tissue,

123
00:05:41,090 --> 00:05:41,730
which is the liver.

124
00:05:42,170 --> 00:05:43,710
Another example of a stable tissue

125
00:05:43,710 --> 00:05:45,790
is the proximal renal tubule of the kidney.

126
00:05:46,210 --> 00:05:48,910
Remember that when patients undergo a tubular necrosis,

127
00:05:48,910 --> 00:05:52,990
ATN, they get destruction of the proximal renal tubular cells,

128
00:05:53,310 --> 00:05:54,730
and those cells can regenerate,

129
00:05:54,810 --> 00:05:56,870
but it takes some time because those are stable cells.

130
00:05:57,290 --> 00:05:59,230
And so often we have to place patients,

131
00:05:59,430 --> 00:06:01,670
well, it's not often, but it occurs on occasion,

132
00:06:02,030 --> 00:06:03,310
where you might have to place a patient

133
00:06:03,310 --> 00:06:05,650
who has ATN, severe ATN, on dialysis

134
00:06:05,650 --> 00:06:07,610
to support them until the kidney

135
00:06:07,610 --> 00:06:11,030
actually regenerates the proximal renal tubule.

136
00:06:11,210 --> 00:06:13,430
And that's another example of a stable tissue.

137
00:06:13,830 --> 00:06:17,170
So again, these are some sort of classic examples

138
00:06:17,170 --> 00:06:17,970
of stable tissue.

139
00:06:18,570 --> 00:06:20,190
The third type of tissue in the human body

140
00:06:20,190 --> 00:06:21,350
is called permanent tissue.

141
00:06:21,450 --> 00:06:23,890
And it's called permanent because it is permanent.

142
00:06:24,070 --> 00:06:26,830
It lacks significant regenerative potential.

143
00:06:27,470 --> 00:06:30,210
And there are three key tissues in the human body

144
00:06:30,210 --> 00:06:32,110
that lack significant regenerative potential

145
00:06:32,110 --> 00:06:35,010
include the myocardium, the skeletal muscle,

146
00:06:35,390 --> 00:06:36,030
and the neurons.

147
00:06:36,670 --> 00:06:40,730
These tissues don't technically have

148
00:06:40,730 --> 00:06:43,410
significant amounts of stem cells.

149
00:06:43,410 --> 00:06:46,810
And therefore, why don't I say these tissues

150
00:06:46,810 --> 00:06:49,190
don't classically have significant amount

151
00:06:49,190 --> 00:06:51,530
of stem cell capability.

152
00:06:51,710 --> 00:06:54,910
And therefore, once they get damaged,

153
00:06:55,030 --> 00:06:56,550
they're not able to regenerate themselves.

154
00:06:56,670 --> 00:06:59,870
And so what's the classic occurrence

155
00:06:59,870 --> 00:07:01,570
if these tissues get damaged?

156
00:07:01,850 --> 00:07:04,030
The classic way by which they would heal

157
00:07:04,030 --> 00:07:04,930
is called repair.

158
00:07:05,430 --> 00:07:06,770
And remember, the idea in repair

159
00:07:06,770 --> 00:07:08,970
is that you can't give back the normal tissue.

160
00:07:08,970 --> 00:07:11,970
So instead, you have to replace that damage

161
00:07:11,970 --> 00:07:13,370
with a fibrous scar.

162
00:07:14,030 --> 00:07:15,290
So when will this occur?

163
00:07:15,390 --> 00:07:18,190
It will occur when a tissue lacks regenerative capacity.

164
00:07:18,430 --> 00:07:21,010
For example, when you get a myocardial infarction

165
00:07:21,010 --> 00:07:21,730
in the heart,

166
00:07:22,470 --> 00:07:24,350
when you get a myocardial infarction in the heart,

167
00:07:24,610 --> 00:07:27,370
the mechanism of healing is always a fibrous scar.

168
00:07:27,530 --> 00:07:29,310
The end result of myocardial infarction

169
00:07:29,310 --> 00:07:30,570
is always a fibrous scar.

170
00:07:30,730 --> 00:07:30,950
Why?

171
00:07:31,310 --> 00:07:33,170
Because the heart does not have the ability

172
00:07:33,170 --> 00:07:34,630
to regenerate tissue.

173
00:07:35,070 --> 00:07:36,330
So this would be a classic picture

174
00:07:36,330 --> 00:07:37,550
that can show up on an examination.

175
00:07:37,550 --> 00:07:40,650
And you can clearly see that this is the heart.

176
00:07:40,810 --> 00:07:42,390
This happens to be the ventricle.

177
00:07:42,690 --> 00:07:45,070
And can you see that there is this white scar

178
00:07:45,070 --> 00:07:48,290
indicating that there was a prior myocardial infarction?

179
00:07:48,550 --> 00:07:50,470
And the reason why we get scarring in the heart,

180
00:07:50,550 --> 00:07:53,690
again, is because the myocardium is a permanent tissue.

181
00:07:54,190 --> 00:07:55,810
Now let me take you back and just remind you

182
00:07:55,810 --> 00:07:57,170
that there is one more example

183
00:07:57,170 --> 00:07:59,990
where you would get repair.

184
00:08:00,270 --> 00:08:02,790
And that would be if the regenerative stem cells are lost.

185
00:08:03,130 --> 00:08:05,410
For example, if you get a paper cut on the skin,

186
00:08:05,410 --> 00:08:07,130
the skin will actually heal completely

187
00:08:07,130 --> 00:08:08,770
and you won't be able to find the paper cut

188
00:08:08,770 --> 00:08:09,750
a couple weeks later.

189
00:08:10,210 --> 00:08:12,250
However, if you cut yourself with a steak knife,

190
00:08:12,970 --> 00:08:14,310
let's just say for example,

191
00:08:14,610 --> 00:08:15,770
you might go deep enough

192
00:08:15,770 --> 00:08:18,710
that you actually take out a piece of skin

193
00:08:18,710 --> 00:08:21,310
such that the basal cells are removed.

194
00:08:21,670 --> 00:08:22,890
And if the basal cells are removed,

195
00:08:22,970 --> 00:08:24,050
then you're going to get a scar

196
00:08:24,050 --> 00:08:27,950
instead of a replacement with skin.

197
00:08:28,190 --> 00:08:30,010
And that's why when you get a very deep cut,

198
00:08:30,090 --> 00:08:32,190
you can actually see a scar form

199
00:08:32,190 --> 00:08:33,750
and you could years later say,

200
00:08:33,850 --> 00:08:34,830
oh, I remember this scar.

201
00:08:34,910 --> 00:08:37,030
I got this when I was cutting steak, for example.

202
00:08:37,570 --> 00:08:39,730
And that would be another example of repair.

203
00:08:39,890 --> 00:08:41,230
And so that's why I've stated here

204
00:08:41,230 --> 00:08:44,070
that it occurs when the regenerative stem cells are lost,

205
00:08:44,170 --> 00:08:46,430
for example, with a deep cut to the skin,

206
00:08:46,750 --> 00:08:49,830
or when the tissue lacks regenerative capacity,

207
00:08:50,010 --> 00:08:51,850
for example, myocardial infarction.

208
00:08:52,430 --> 00:08:54,150
All right, so both of those are kind of high yield.

209
00:08:54,690 --> 00:08:58,070
Now, when repair is occurring,

210
00:08:58,210 --> 00:08:59,110
repair means what?

211
00:08:59,110 --> 00:09:03,030
It refers to the replacement of the tissue with scar.

212
00:09:03,490 --> 00:09:05,070
The initial phase of repair

213
00:09:05,070 --> 00:09:07,170
will be called granulation tissue.

214
00:09:08,250 --> 00:09:09,970
That's the very initial phase of repair.

215
00:09:10,270 --> 00:09:13,270
And what does granulation tissue consist of?

216
00:09:13,750 --> 00:09:17,250
Fibroblasts, capillaries, and myofibroblasts.

217
00:09:17,630 --> 00:09:20,550
There are three major components of granulation tissue.

218
00:09:20,950 --> 00:09:23,550
Fibroblasts, capillaries, and myofibroblasts.

219
00:09:23,850 --> 00:09:25,130
Now, what do the capillaries do?

220
00:09:25,210 --> 00:09:26,290
They provide nutrients.

221
00:09:26,770 --> 00:09:28,330
What do the fibroblasts do?

222
00:09:28,330 --> 00:09:30,050
They deposit type 3 collagen.

223
00:09:30,530 --> 00:09:31,990
And what do the myofibroblasts do?

224
00:09:32,150 --> 00:09:33,750
They contract the wound down.

225
00:09:34,230 --> 00:09:36,150
Each of these are very important to be familiar with.

226
00:09:36,210 --> 00:09:37,310
But let me take a step back.

227
00:09:37,670 --> 00:09:39,770
First of all, when we talk about granulation tissue,

228
00:09:39,890 --> 00:09:41,170
I want to make it very clear

229
00:09:41,170 --> 00:09:44,050
that granulation tissue is different than a granuloma.

230
00:09:45,030 --> 00:09:47,610
Unfortunately, sometimes students confuse these two terms

231
00:09:47,610 --> 00:09:49,090
because they sound a little bit similar.

232
00:09:49,550 --> 00:09:50,490
What is granuloma?

233
00:09:51,110 --> 00:09:53,310
Granuloma is a subtype of chronic inflammation.

234
00:09:54,010 --> 00:09:55,510
And what's the hallmark of granuloma?

235
00:09:55,510 --> 00:09:57,630
Granuloma, epithelioid, histiocyte.

236
00:09:57,870 --> 00:09:59,230
And how is a granuloma formed?

237
00:09:59,890 --> 00:10:03,190
IL-12 from the macrophage hits the CD4-positive T cell,

238
00:10:03,270 --> 00:10:07,010
converting it to a helper-type, TH1-type cell.

239
00:10:07,370 --> 00:10:09,650
And the TH1 cell secretes interferon gamma

240
00:10:09,650 --> 00:10:14,450
that gives the macrophage an epithelioid histiocyte appearance.

241
00:10:14,790 --> 00:10:14,910
Okay?

242
00:10:14,910 --> 00:10:19,490
And so granuloma is distinct from granulation tissue.

243
00:10:19,610 --> 00:10:22,210
Granulation tissue is the initial phase of repair

244
00:10:22,210 --> 00:10:23,410
when you're forming a scar.

245
00:10:24,170 --> 00:10:27,270
Now, granulation tissue consists of fibroblasts,

246
00:10:27,370 --> 00:10:29,490
and the fibroblasts deposit type 3 collagen.

247
00:10:30,230 --> 00:10:31,650
It consists of capillaries,

248
00:10:31,750 --> 00:10:33,310
and the capillaries provide the nutrients.

249
00:10:33,890 --> 00:10:36,790
And then the granulation tissue also consists of myofibroblasts,

250
00:10:36,850 --> 00:10:38,190
and these contract down the wound.

251
00:10:38,190 --> 00:10:42,910
And I'll talk about this in a little bit more detail in a few slides,

252
00:10:42,990 --> 00:10:45,390
but let's see a picture first, or let's look at a picture first.

253
00:10:45,670 --> 00:10:47,170
So this is granulation tissue,

254
00:10:47,310 --> 00:10:49,210
and you can see that this granulation tissue

255
00:10:49,210 --> 00:10:51,390
is comprised of these blood vessels.

256
00:10:51,810 --> 00:10:53,590
And in addition to these blood vessels,

257
00:10:53,750 --> 00:10:56,390
you can clearly see that there's this deposition of collagen

258
00:10:56,390 --> 00:10:58,990
and the presence of some of these fibroblasts,

259
00:10:59,270 --> 00:11:01,430
which are also part of the granulation tissue.

260
00:11:01,850 --> 00:11:06,310
So again, this is the classic histologic finding of granulation tissue.

261
00:11:06,310 --> 00:11:11,270
Now, it's important to recall or remember or to learn

262
00:11:11,270 --> 00:11:15,570
that eventually granulation tissue will produce a scar.

263
00:11:15,830 --> 00:11:16,790
Okay, so that's the end result.

264
00:11:16,870 --> 00:11:19,370
Like I said, granulation tissue is the early form

265
00:11:19,370 --> 00:11:21,090
of what will eventually become a scar.

266
00:11:21,610 --> 00:11:25,050
And one of the key features that distinguishes

267
00:11:25,050 --> 00:11:26,550
granulation tissue from a scar

268
00:11:26,550 --> 00:11:29,770
is that when you have a scar,

269
00:11:30,250 --> 00:11:32,890
the type 3 collagen of the granulation tissue

270
00:11:32,890 --> 00:11:35,030
is replaced with type 1 collagen.

271
00:11:35,030 --> 00:11:38,050
Maybe this is a good point to take a step back

272
00:11:38,050 --> 00:11:40,750
and just remind ourselves of collagen

273
00:11:40,750 --> 00:11:42,170
and its different subtypes.

274
00:11:42,250 --> 00:11:43,790
So I'm going to actually clear out the board here

275
00:11:43,790 --> 00:11:44,910
and give you a whiteboard.

276
00:11:45,610 --> 00:11:49,630
Can you remember a few important points of collagen number 1?

277
00:11:50,350 --> 00:11:52,650
One important collagen is type 1 collagen.

278
00:11:53,230 --> 00:11:55,410
And do you remember what type 1 collagen is seen in?

279
00:11:55,850 --> 00:11:56,870
It's seen in bone.

280
00:11:57,370 --> 00:11:59,270
And one of the ways that people remember that

281
00:11:59,270 --> 00:12:01,310
is that when you think about bone,

282
00:12:02,410 --> 00:12:04,650
it has the word 1 within it.

283
00:12:04,710 --> 00:12:06,150
So that reminds you of type 1 collagen.

284
00:12:06,730 --> 00:12:08,950
But what I'd like you to know is that

285
00:12:08,950 --> 00:12:11,210
one of the key features of type 1 collagen

286
00:12:11,210 --> 00:12:12,330
is its strength.

287
00:12:12,530 --> 00:12:15,210
It has strong tensile strength.

288
00:12:15,410 --> 00:12:20,990
It provides support and it has the tensile strength

289
00:12:20,990 --> 00:12:24,570
that is particular to type 1 collagen.

290
00:12:25,590 --> 00:12:30,790
Okay, type 2 collagen is predominantly seen in cartilage.

291
00:12:32,570 --> 00:12:34,890
And one of the ways by which people remember that

292
00:12:34,890 --> 00:12:35,990
is cartilage.

293
00:12:36,310 --> 00:12:38,710
So some people say cartilage, type 2 collagen.

294
00:12:39,390 --> 00:12:41,790
Type 3 collagen, if I had to summarize it,

295
00:12:42,050 --> 00:12:43,030
the way I remember it,

296
00:12:43,230 --> 00:12:47,630
is that type 3 collagen is sort of classic for its pliability.

297
00:12:48,130 --> 00:12:48,870
It's pliable.

298
00:12:49,390 --> 00:12:50,830
And so where do we see type 3 collagen?

299
00:12:50,970 --> 00:12:53,010
Blood vessels, which need the ability to stretch.

300
00:12:53,390 --> 00:12:56,190
We see type 3 collagen in granulation tissue

301
00:12:56,190 --> 00:12:59,010
because granulation tissue is sort of the initial phase of scar.

302
00:12:59,130 --> 00:13:02,370
It needs to be able to stretch and mold into a final scar.

303
00:13:02,790 --> 00:13:04,490
And we see it in embryonic tissue.

304
00:13:04,690 --> 00:13:07,590
There's a lot of type 3 collagen in embryonic skin, for example,

305
00:13:07,710 --> 00:13:10,350
because the baby's growing and needs to be able to stretch.

306
00:13:10,770 --> 00:13:12,530
So if I had to summarize type 3 collagen,

307
00:13:12,710 --> 00:13:14,490
I would summarize it with the word pliability.

308
00:13:14,490 --> 00:13:21,570
And then type 4 collagen is predominantly seen in basement membrane.

309
00:13:22,050 --> 00:13:25,070
So these are some high yields concerning collagen.

310
00:13:25,190 --> 00:13:26,370
I just wanted to give you that.

311
00:13:26,630 --> 00:13:28,070
But I want to take you back and remind you

312
00:13:28,070 --> 00:13:31,470
that granulation tissue has type 3 collagen initially, right?

313
00:13:31,550 --> 00:13:33,670
And that's produced by the fibroblasts.

314
00:13:33,730 --> 00:13:36,090
And the type 3 collagen is important because it's pliable.

315
00:13:36,490 --> 00:13:38,970
But eventually, you want to replace it with type 1 collagen.

316
00:13:38,970 --> 00:13:44,610
And you want type 1 collagen because that really provides the stability necessary in the final scar.

317
00:13:45,590 --> 00:13:50,530
Important to know in high yield for examinations is that when we want to get rid of the type 3 collagen

318
00:13:50,530 --> 00:13:55,010
and eventually convert it to type 1 collagen, we're going to use collagenase,

319
00:13:55,090 --> 00:13:56,790
which is going to eat up that type 3 collagen.

320
00:13:57,350 --> 00:14:04,050
And very high yield, zinc, is a cofactor for collagenase in order to remove type 3 collagen

321
00:14:04,050 --> 00:14:06,010
and replace it with type 1, okay?

322
00:14:06,090 --> 00:14:07,110
So that's in biochemistry.

323
00:14:07,270 --> 00:14:10,330
And you'll see we're going to hit on that one more time before we end this lecture.

324
00:14:11,250 --> 00:14:13,510
What is the mechanism of regeneration and repair?

325
00:14:14,010 --> 00:14:20,510
Well, the mechanism of regeneration and repair is that it is mediated by paracrine signaling

326
00:14:20,510 --> 00:14:23,910
by growth factors.

327
00:14:23,910 --> 00:14:28,350
All right, and it's an interaction of these factors with receptors that results in growth.

328
00:14:28,530 --> 00:14:31,550
And all I'm trying to really highlight here is that there are factors.

329
00:14:31,950 --> 00:14:34,470
The factors are secreted, for example, by macrophages.

330
00:14:34,670 --> 00:14:36,650
And the factors will then bind receptors.

331
00:14:36,850 --> 00:14:42,250
And it's these receptor-factor interactions that will result in regeneration and repair.

332
00:14:42,390 --> 00:14:43,410
That's what I'm trying to highlight.

333
00:14:43,970 --> 00:14:48,170
And the reason I'm even bringing up this discussion is because examiners like to go after

334
00:14:48,170 --> 00:14:51,650
different subtypes of molecules that are important for the process.

335
00:14:51,650 --> 00:14:56,850
For example, TGF-alpha is an important epithelial and fibroblast growth factor.

336
00:14:57,870 --> 00:15:02,630
All right, TGF-beta is an important fibroblast growth factor, and it inhibits inflammation.

337
00:15:03,230 --> 00:15:08,230
But this should not be so hard for you to remember because if you go back to one of our earlier

338
00:15:08,230 --> 00:15:15,230
lectures when we talked about acute inflammation and macrophages, remember that I said that macrophages,

339
00:15:15,570 --> 00:15:20,390
if they come in and they see that there is no longer a need for inflammation, they'll sort

340
00:15:20,390 --> 00:15:22,930
of shut down inflammation and turn on the healing response.

341
00:15:23,390 --> 00:15:28,110
And they said there's two major molecules you should be familiar with, IL-10 and TGF-beta.

342
00:15:28,450 --> 00:15:30,590
Well, here is exactly that same TGF-beta.

343
00:15:30,890 --> 00:15:32,190
So what is TGF-beta going to do?

344
00:15:32,230 --> 00:15:36,270
It's going to cause fibroblast growth, which is important for healing, and it's going to

345
00:15:36,270 --> 00:15:39,650
inhibit inflammation to shut down that inflammatory process.

346
00:15:40,250 --> 00:15:43,170
Another important example would be platelet-derived growth factor.

347
00:15:43,170 --> 00:15:45,450
Now, again, no rocket science.

348
00:15:45,970 --> 00:15:47,030
What do platelets do?

349
00:15:47,130 --> 00:15:50,830
Platelets help to seal a blood vessel that's been damaged, correct?

350
00:15:51,210 --> 00:15:56,550
So if platelets are activated, one of the important things that they release is platelet-derived

351
00:15:56,550 --> 00:15:57,090
growth factor.

352
00:15:57,170 --> 00:15:58,710
And what is that growth factor going to do?

353
00:15:59,070 --> 00:16:01,390
Well, it's going to help the endothelium regrow.

354
00:16:01,950 --> 00:16:05,010
It's going to help the smooth muscle of the blood vessel wall regrow.

355
00:16:05,410 --> 00:16:08,030
And it's going to help fibroblasts come in for the healing.

356
00:16:08,450 --> 00:16:10,210
And so that's platelet-derived growth factor.

357
00:16:10,630 --> 00:16:13,910
Here's one that's very important to be aware of, fibroblast growth factor.

358
00:16:14,010 --> 00:16:18,590
And I would say it's very important because although it's called fibroblast growth factor,

359
00:16:18,970 --> 00:16:22,130
it actually is a very strong angiogenic factor.

360
00:16:22,450 --> 00:16:24,350
Angiogenic helps to grow blood vessels.

361
00:16:24,890 --> 00:16:29,390
And so it's important to know this one because it sort of is different than the rest in the

362
00:16:29,390 --> 00:16:32,590
sense that name doesn't necessarily indicate what it does.

363
00:16:32,950 --> 00:16:37,590
It's also important for skeletal development, but we'll talk about that in your musculoskeletal

364
00:16:37,590 --> 00:16:37,930
lectures.

365
00:16:37,930 --> 00:16:44,690
And finally, vascular endothelial growth factor, vascular endothelial growth factor, obviously,

366
00:16:45,130 --> 00:16:47,250
is going to be important for angiogenesis.

367
00:16:48,170 --> 00:16:54,110
Now, when we have cutaneous healing, that is healing of the skin, there are two ways by

368
00:16:54,110 --> 00:16:54,930
which that can occur.

369
00:16:55,410 --> 00:16:57,510
Primary intention, which is what?

370
00:16:57,810 --> 00:16:58,950
We take the wound edges.

371
00:16:59,050 --> 00:17:00,550
For example, you get a big cut.

372
00:17:00,550 --> 00:17:05,910
You go to the ER, and they take the wound edges, and they use suture to bring them together.

373
00:17:06,330 --> 00:17:08,150
Now, why would you want to bring the two edges together?

374
00:17:08,370 --> 00:17:11,550
Because that will result in minimal scar formation, right?

375
00:17:11,630 --> 00:17:15,270
Because the skin has the ability to heal, and it'll fill in the defect.

376
00:17:16,110 --> 00:17:20,530
Another way by which we can get cutaneous healing is called secondary intention.

377
00:17:20,670 --> 00:17:21,890
And what's secondary intention?

378
00:17:21,890 --> 00:17:27,070
Basically, we don't let the edges be approximated, so we don't stitch them together.

379
00:17:27,450 --> 00:17:31,730
Instead, the tissue remains open, and granulation tissue fills in the defect.

380
00:17:32,410 --> 00:17:35,630
Now, when granulation tissue fills in the defect, what's going to happen?

381
00:17:36,450 --> 00:17:37,910
You're going to get a big scar.

382
00:17:38,390 --> 00:17:44,450
But more importantly, at least for examinations, is that the granulation tissue will cause contraction

383
00:17:44,450 --> 00:17:46,930
of that wound slightly, all right?

384
00:17:47,050 --> 00:17:51,950
So there could be a potential examination question where they ask you that a patient has a large

385
00:17:51,950 --> 00:17:57,550
wound that is healed by secondary intention, and six weeks later, you notice that the wound

386
00:17:57,550 --> 00:17:59,890
has significantly reduced in size.

387
00:18:00,230 --> 00:18:04,630
And then they can ask you, what's the mechanism by which this wound actually reduces in size?

388
00:18:04,830 --> 00:18:07,730
And the answer is the myofibroblast.

389
00:18:08,150 --> 00:18:08,970
Now, let's go back.

390
00:18:09,250 --> 00:18:11,410
Remember that granulation tissue has three components.

391
00:18:11,410 --> 00:18:16,530
It has capillaries, it has fibroblasts that lay down type 3 collagen, and then it has

392
00:18:16,530 --> 00:18:17,670
myofibroblasts.

393
00:18:17,770 --> 00:18:20,570
And the myo means that they have some contractile function.

394
00:18:21,190 --> 00:18:26,270
So the myofibroblasts have the ability to contract the wound and make it smaller.

395
00:18:26,750 --> 00:18:28,790
And that's actually high yield for examination.

396
00:18:29,130 --> 00:18:31,510
So that's why I actually brought this topic up to begin with.

397
00:18:32,870 --> 00:18:33,770
Delayed wound healing.

398
00:18:33,890 --> 00:18:34,910
What is delayed wound healing?

399
00:18:35,030 --> 00:18:38,650
This is where a patient has a wound, but it doesn't heal in the appropriate time.

400
00:18:39,210 --> 00:18:40,670
Infection is the most common cause.

401
00:18:40,670 --> 00:18:46,290
If there is infection, there'll be chronic inflammation, or there'll be continued inflammation,

402
00:18:46,790 --> 00:18:51,610
and the continued inflammation will then impair the healing of the wound.

403
00:18:51,890 --> 00:18:53,990
So that's the most common cause of delayed wound healing.

404
00:18:54,510 --> 00:18:56,470
Another cause would be vitamin C deficiency.

405
00:18:56,930 --> 00:18:57,950
Why do we need vitamin C?

406
00:18:58,050 --> 00:18:58,750
Well, let's go back.

407
00:18:59,110 --> 00:19:06,010
Remind ourselves, when collagen is produced by the cell, it is produced as separate alpha

408
00:19:06,010 --> 00:19:07,010
chains.

409
00:19:07,010 --> 00:19:15,170
The alpha chains wrap around each other, and the final result of the alpha chains wrapping

410
00:19:15,170 --> 00:19:17,830
around each other is something called procollagen.

411
00:19:18,390 --> 00:19:23,970
Now, procollagen will eventually then be secreted outside of the cell, and it will be cross-linked

412
00:19:23,970 --> 00:19:24,790
outside of the cell.

413
00:19:24,790 --> 00:19:30,010
In order to cross-link the procollagen, one of the things that has to occur before cross-linking

414
00:19:30,010 --> 00:19:35,530
can occur is that you need to hydroxylate the proline and lysine residues of procollagen.

415
00:19:35,530 --> 00:19:40,310
This is getting a little complex, so maybe let me just remind you that when you have collagen,

416
00:19:40,830 --> 00:19:46,890
collagen's normal amino acid structure is glycine, and then XY.

417
00:19:47,170 --> 00:19:48,270
It's called GlyXY.

418
00:19:48,710 --> 00:19:53,790
So glycine is usually one amino acid, and then the next amino acid is usually, this X

419
00:19:53,790 --> 00:19:57,150
refers to proline or lysine, but usually proline.

420
00:19:57,150 --> 00:20:02,650
And then this Y is usually going to be proline or lysine, and that proline or lysine will actually

421
00:20:02,650 --> 00:20:06,190
get hydroxylated, so that it'll get an OH group added to it.

422
00:20:06,590 --> 00:20:08,290
Now, how does that OH group get added?

423
00:20:08,430 --> 00:20:14,850
Well, vitamin C is necessary to add that OH group to create hydroxyproline or hydroxylysine.

424
00:20:15,190 --> 00:20:16,530
Now, why do you want that OH group?

425
00:20:16,850 --> 00:20:22,290
Because this OH group in the sequence of collagen is necessary so that when you want to cross-link

426
00:20:22,290 --> 00:20:30,590
another collagen molecule, it's cross-linking occurs via the OH groups, and that then allows

427
00:20:30,590 --> 00:20:34,530
procollagen to be strengthened to the final collagen molecule.

428
00:20:35,150 --> 00:20:42,430
And so vitamin C is a necessary chemical, or we can say a necessary vitamin, for the strengthening

429
00:20:42,430 --> 00:20:43,330
of collagen.

430
00:20:43,710 --> 00:20:44,890
So that should be clear now.

431
00:20:44,890 --> 00:20:48,830
And that's why I say that vitamin C can result in delayed wound healing.

432
00:20:48,830 --> 00:20:53,050
Another important molecule that could result in delayed wound healing is lack of copper.

433
00:20:53,390 --> 00:20:58,590
If there's a copper deficiency, there can be delayed wound healing, and that's because

434
00:20:58,590 --> 00:21:05,730
the enzyme that cross-links lysine and hydroxylysine to form that stable collagen, one of the enzymes

435
00:21:05,730 --> 00:21:10,730
is called lysol oxidase, and lysol oxidase requires copper as a cofactor.

436
00:21:10,850 --> 00:21:15,190
So if there's a lack of copper, we won't be able to cross-link our collagen, and that would

437
00:21:15,190 --> 00:21:16,810
give us delayed wound healing.

438
00:21:16,810 --> 00:21:20,430
And the final deficiency that could result in delayed wound healing is zinc.

439
00:21:20,650 --> 00:21:21,470
Why do we need zinc?

440
00:21:21,870 --> 00:21:22,830
We've already talked about it.

441
00:21:23,610 --> 00:21:28,050
Well, remember that granulation tissue normally contains type 3 collagen, and we need to convert

442
00:21:28,050 --> 00:21:32,290
that type 3 collagen to type 1 collagen to give the final strength of the scar.

443
00:21:32,690 --> 00:21:40,810
But we need zinc in order to be able to properly do that, because the collagenase that removes

444
00:21:40,810 --> 00:21:44,750
type 1 collagen actually requires zinc as a cofactor.

445
00:21:44,750 --> 00:21:46,350
So that's that.

446
00:21:46,430 --> 00:21:49,590
And these are all high yield, because they all tie into biochemistry.

447
00:21:50,270 --> 00:21:55,370
Now other causes of delayed wound healing include foreign body, because that would induce

448
00:21:55,370 --> 00:21:56,350
chronic inflammation.

449
00:21:56,970 --> 00:22:00,630
Ischemia, because you're not going to get good blood supply to the tissue, so that would

450
00:22:00,630 --> 00:22:03,210
then decrease the ability to heal the tissue.

451
00:22:03,770 --> 00:22:06,270
Diabetes is associated with delayed wound healing.

452
00:22:06,270 --> 00:22:07,430
And malnutrition.

453
00:22:07,550 --> 00:22:11,450
Again, if there's not good nutrition, there's not going to be the necessary building blocks

454
00:22:11,450 --> 00:22:14,030
to allow wound healing to occur.

455
00:22:14,990 --> 00:22:16,810
Dehistence is a complication of wound healing.

456
00:22:17,110 --> 00:22:18,930
Basically, it's when the wound opens.

457
00:22:19,170 --> 00:22:20,350
There's a rupture of a wound.

458
00:22:20,630 --> 00:22:24,850
And it's most commonly seen after abdominal surgery, where the actual wound can tear open.

459
00:22:25,050 --> 00:22:26,130
And that's called dehiscence.

460
00:22:27,110 --> 00:22:27,930
Hypertrophic scar.

461
00:22:28,090 --> 00:22:29,130
What's a hypertrophic scar?

462
00:22:29,130 --> 00:22:33,250
It is excess production of scar tissue, but it is localized to the wound.

463
00:22:33,610 --> 00:22:34,670
Here's a classic example.

464
00:22:35,070 --> 00:22:41,210
This patient had a scar, or had a wound, but notice that the scar is a little thick and

465
00:22:41,210 --> 00:22:41,670
prominent.

466
00:22:42,310 --> 00:22:45,130
And so this is an example of a hypertrophic scar.

467
00:22:45,630 --> 00:22:48,910
The hypertrophic scar is predominantly made of type 1 collagen.

468
00:22:49,130 --> 00:22:51,510
So there was granulation tissue, which was type 3.

469
00:22:51,930 --> 00:22:56,670
The collagenase plus zinc came in, converted the type 3 collagen to type 1 collagen.

470
00:22:56,670 --> 00:23:04,470
And so this is excess production of type 1 collagen as part of an aberration of normal scarring.

471
00:23:04,730 --> 00:23:06,290
So that's called a hypertrophic scar.

472
00:23:07,150 --> 00:23:11,430
Another example of a problem with wound healing is called a keloid.

473
00:23:12,070 --> 00:23:13,350
And this is very high yield.

474
00:23:13,550 --> 00:23:14,730
It likes to show up on exams.

475
00:23:15,230 --> 00:23:20,050
So keloid is excess production of scar tissue, but it's way out of proportion to the wound.

476
00:23:20,310 --> 00:23:21,690
Way out of proportion to the wound.

477
00:23:21,690 --> 00:23:27,150
And another important feature of a keloid is that it is characterized by type 3 collagen.

478
00:23:27,490 --> 00:23:30,150
There's an excess of type 3 collagen in the keloid.

479
00:23:30,670 --> 00:23:32,210
There's a genetic predisposition.

480
00:23:32,370 --> 00:23:34,070
It's more commonly seen in African Americans.

481
00:23:34,550 --> 00:23:36,570
And the classic location is the earlobe.

482
00:23:37,650 --> 00:23:43,830
So these are some important facts or features of a keloid.

483
00:23:43,930 --> 00:23:47,630
You need to be familiar with these for the purposes of board examinations.

484
00:23:48,290 --> 00:23:51,010
And here is a classic picture.

485
00:23:51,010 --> 00:23:55,210
So this patient, for example, may have undergone a tiny little ear piercing.

486
00:23:55,610 --> 00:24:01,590
And in response to that tiny ear piercing, this large tumor-like mass developed.

487
00:24:01,970 --> 00:24:03,310
And so this is the keloid.

488
00:24:03,490 --> 00:24:04,970
And so, again, classic features.

489
00:24:05,090 --> 00:24:08,370
The keloid predominantly contains type 3 collagen.

490
00:24:08,530 --> 00:24:09,350
You need to know that.

491
00:24:09,850 --> 00:24:13,290
It's way out of proportion to the wound.

492
00:24:13,390 --> 00:24:14,570
A tiny little ear piercing.

493
00:24:14,690 --> 00:24:17,630
It results in a large tumor-like mass.

494
00:24:17,630 --> 00:24:23,990
And then, important to also know about keloids, genetic predisposition in African Americans.

495
00:24:24,590 --> 00:24:26,710
And also classically seen in the earlobe.

496
00:24:26,790 --> 00:24:29,330
So those are some of the high yields concerning the keloid.

497
00:24:29,710 --> 00:24:35,010
So that really ties up the Chapter 2 discussion of inflammation.

498
00:24:35,010 --> 00:24:38,930
So a lot to be familiar with.

499
00:24:39,070 --> 00:24:42,710
